What is the historical context of the GI Bill and its relationship to the 9/11 attacks?
The GI Bill has its roots in the Servicemen's Readjustment Act of 1944, also known as the GI Bill of Rights, which was signed into law on June 22, 1944. This legislation was enacted to provide benefits to veterans of World War II, aiming to avoid the post-war confusion and controversy that arose after World War I.

Fast-forward to the 21st century, the Post-9/11 GI Bill was signed into law in July 2008, creating a new education benefits program for service members who served on active duty for 90 or more days since September 10, 2001. This new GI Bill was a response to the needs of veterans who served in the aftermath of the September 11 attacks, which occurred on September 11, 2001.

In essence, the GI Bill has evolved over time to address the changing needs of veterans, with the Post-9/11 GI Bill being a direct response to the sacrifices made by service members in the wars that followed the 9/11 attacks.
