The Jewish calendar is a lunisolar calendar, meaning it's based on both the cycles of the moon and the sun. It has 12 or 13 months in a year, depending on the number of days in the solar year. The months are:

1. Nisan (March/April)
2. Iyar (April/May)
3. Sivan (May/June)
4. Tamuz (June/July)
5. Av (July/August)
6. Elul (August/September)
7. Tishrei (September/October)
8. Marheshvan (October/November)
9. Kislev (November/December)
10. Tevet (December/January)
11. Shevat (January/February)
12. Adar (February/March)
13. Adar II (in leap years, added between Adar and Nisan)

The Jewish calendar is about 11 days shorter than the solar year, so an extra month is added every 2-3 years to keep it in sync with the solar year. This extra month is called Adar II.

In terms of organization, the Jewish calendar is divided into:

* Weeks (Sabbaths)
* Months (as listed above)
* Seasons:
	+ Spring: Nisan to Iyar
	+ Summer: Sivan to Av
	+ Fall: Elul to Tishrei
	+ Winter: Marheshvan to Shevat
	+ Spring:
