What were the key arguments presented by the plaintiffs and defendants in the Brown v. Board of Education case, and how did they relate to the 14th Amendment?
Based on the provided context, the key arguments presented by the plaintiffs in the Brown v. Board of Education case were that racial segregation in education, despite being labeled as "separate but equal," was inherently unequal and perpetuated inferior accommodations, services, and treatment for black Americans. They argued that this system violated the Fourteenth Amendment's guarantee of equal protection of the laws.

The defendants, on the other hand, relied on the precedent set by the U.S. Supreme Court in Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), which had upheld the "separate but equal" doctrine. They argued that as long as the separate facilities for the separate races were equal, segregation did not violate the Fourteenth Amendment.

The plaintiffs' arguments were supported by the research of John Aubrey Davis Sr. and his team, who compiled evidence that the Fourteenth Amendment prohibited racial discrimination. The Supreme Court ultimately ruled in favor of the plaintiffs, holding that "separate educational facilities are inherently unequal" and that the plaintiffs were deprived of the equal protection of the laws guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment.
