What was the outcome of the Brown v. Board of Education case at the district court level, and what were the subsequent appeals?
The District Court ruled in favor of the Board of Education, citing the U.S. Supreme Court precedent set in "Plessy v. Ferguson", which had upheld a state law requiring "separate but equal" segregated facilities for blacks and whites in railway cars. However, the three-judge District Court panel found that segregation in public education has a detrimental effect.

The case was then appealed to the U.S. Supreme Court, where it was consolidated with four other cases, including Briggs v. Elliott, Davis v. County School Board of Prince Edward County, and Mendez v. Westminster. The Supreme Court ultimately ruled in favor of the plaintiffs, declaring that segregation in public education is unconstitutional and violating the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.
