What is the historical context and purpose of the Royal Flying Doctor Service?
The Royal Flying Doctor Service (RFDS) was established by Reverend John Flynn on 15 May 1928 in Cloncurry, Queensland, Australia. Its historical context and purpose are rooted in providing medical support to isolated communities in rural and remote areas of Australia. Flynn's vision was to use radios and aircraft to connect doctors and patients in these areas, addressing the challenges of accessing healthcare due to vast distances. The RFDS aimed to fly out to patients, treat them, and transport them to hospitals if necessary, thereby bridging the gap in healthcare access for those living in remote locations.
