The historical context and purpose behind the 'Keep Calm and Carry On' phrase is rooted in World War II. The phrase was produced by the British government in 1939 as part of a series of three "Home Publicity" posters intended to raise the morale of the British public in the event of a wartime disaster, such as mass bombing of major cities using high explosives and poison gas. The poster was designed to be distributed to strengthen morale and was intended for the general public, with the slogan "Keep Calm and Carry On" appearing under a representation of a "Tudor Crown" (a symbol of the state). The phrase was meant to be a rallying cry to encourage the public to remain calm and continue with their daily lives, even in the face of potential danger.
